## Context
Each document in the chain opens with an Intro that restates the authority
chain: rulings land in DECISIONS.md first, the other documents are edited to
match, DESIGN.md owns the design numbers, and so on. Read one document at a
time this is orientation. Read end to end — which is how a newcomer, a
reviewer, or the author in six months reads it — the same paragraph appears
four times, soon five once FIELD-REVIEW.md joins.

The repository's own rule names this defect: "One statement per fact:
thresholds, rules, and open questions live in their owning document and
everywhere else is a pointer. Duplicated numbers drift — this repo's most
expensive recurring defect." The authority chain is a rule, so it falls
under that sentence, and four copies of it can drift the same way four
copies of a number can.

The author's reading (2026-08-29): the shared part should probably become a
pointer to README.md, which already carries the authoritative-chain
sentence.

## The tension with 0036, stated plainly
0036 has a ticked exit criterion: "Every preamble replaced by an Intro
section." That was the right move and this ticket does not reverse it. The
Intro sections stay. What moves out of them is only the portion that is
identical across documents — the description of the chain as a whole. What
stays is the portion specific to that document: what THIS document owns,
what it does not own, and where its own authority ends.

A useful test for each Intro sentence: would it be equally true if pasted
into a sibling document? If yes, it is chain description and belongs in one
place. If no, it is this document's own statement and stays.

Blocked by 0036 because 0036's last criterion is the author's acceptance of
the rewritten voice, and these are the same paragraphs.

## Relevant files
- `README.md` — the "authoritative chain is:" sentence, the canonical site
- `spec/REQUIREMENTS.md`, `CONSTRAINTS.md`, `DESIGN.md` — Intro sections
- `spec/DECISIONS.md` — its italic front matter states the chain too; the front
  matter is NOT a ratified entry and may be edited, unlike the entries below
- `spec/FIELD-REVIEW.md` — the newest Intro; include it

## Actions
1. Diff the four Intros against each other and mark each sentence as
   chain-description or document-specific, using the paste test above.
2. Confirm README.md's sentence is the best canonical statement, and improve
   it if the Intros contain a sharper phrasing worth promoting.
3. Replace the duplicated portion with a one-line pointer. The pointer must
   name README.md by file, so it survives a reader entering mid-document.
4. Keep each Intro's document-specific half intact and readable standing
   alone. An Intro reduced to nothing but a pointer has gone too far — a
   reader entering at DESIGN.md still needs to learn what DESIGN.md owns
   without leaving the file.
5. Add the reading protocol to README.md's canonical statement, in one
   paragraph: each document in the chain has its own organising principle —
   DECISIONS.md chronological because ratification is chronological,
   REQUIREMENTS.md and CONSTRAINTS.md enumerative because they are
   checklists, DESIGN.md architectural because architecture has structure.
   Say so and say why. This is settled (author, 2026-08-29): the differing
   spines stay, because they follow from the authority separation rather than
   from carelessness, and forcing one spine would cost the property that
   makes the chain worth having. The defect was never the difference; it was
   that a reader meeting it is never told. Nothing else to do about spines.

## Test
A check that fails when the same sentence, normalised, appears in more than
one chain document. Red first: it must fire on the current tree, which is
the positive control for the whole ticket.

## Verification
- [ ] Each Intro still tells a mid-document arrival what that document owns
- [ ] The chain is described once, in README.md, with its reading protocol
- [ ] No pointer is a bare cross-reference with no local context

## Invariants
- 0036's Intro sections survive as sections; only their shared paragraph
  moves.
- No ratified DECISIONS.md entry is edited; its front matter is fair game.
- Authority itself is unchanged. This is deduplication, not a re-assignment
  of who owns what.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The authority chain is stated once and pointed at from each Intro
- [ ] README.md names each document's organising principle and why it differs
- [ ] The duplicate-sentence guard is in place and fires on regression
- [ ] `make check` passes
